            <ArticleTitle>An emerging and devastating acute abdomen in a patient under peritoneal dialysis: encapsulating peritoneal sclerosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>245.e3-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajem.2007.03.034</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Peritoneal dialysis (PD) has been applied to patients with end-stage renal disease for more than 2 decades. It should raise physicians' concern about the serious complications of prolonged PD therapy, particularly encapsulating peritoneal sclerosis (EPS), the most potentially life-threatening one. The prevalence and mortality rate of EPS increase as PD duration increases. We report a case of EPS presented with blood-tinged effluents and abdominal pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
